WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.611 --> 00:00:05.794
Hi there! Steve Kaufmann here again.
Time to do another little chat.

00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:11.109
You know, I put up that
last little discussion,

00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:14.941
and I notice that, you
know how it is on Youtube,

00:00:14.941 --> 00:00:22.005
if you look up the...
some other, some... web video chat

00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:26.731
you have just done you will
see some related... youtubes.

00:00:26.731 --> 00:00:29.565
And so I saw to...
Some there were quite interesting.

00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:34.093
One was something by
a British professor,

00:00:34.315 --> 00:00:38.466
or a language teacher who talked
about language learning in Web 2.0.

00:00:40.224 --> 00:00:45.745
And you might find that, if you look up
one of my videos you might see his.

00:00:47.688 --> 00:00:51.069
But my impression of his presentation,
and it was a 9 minute presentation,

00:00:51.069 --> 00:00:54.310
it was much appreciated
by all the teachers.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.029
But I think he misunderstood
and he saw Web 2.0 as...

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:02.069
as yet, another tool by
which the teachers can...

00:01:02.744 --> 00:01:06.863
sort of control the activities
of students and learners.

00:01:08.346 --> 00:01:11.568
And I think Web 2.0 is about
the learners taking over.

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:16.955
And the differences between learners
and teachers are kind of blurred.

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:21.386
Everyone contributes, everyone is a
consumer, everyone is a producer,

00:01:21.386 --> 00:01:24.823
people made sound files, people learn
from what they are interested in

00:01:24.823 --> 00:01:27.188
They don't have to follow
the teacher's agenda;

00:01:27.188 --> 00:01:31.791
they don't have to do, sort of, make-work
projects that the teacher assigns them.

00:01:32.284 --> 00:01:35.045
Ideally, they get out of
the classroom, which is a

00:01:35.045 --> 00:01:38.776
relatively inefficient
place to learn languages.

00:01:39.252 --> 00:01:45.386
So... But you might  have a look at it. I
was not that impressed with what was there.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.490
The other...
There were a series of a...

00:01:50.156 --> 00:01:56.329
of a webcams by a professor,
Alexander Arguelles.

00:01:56.329 --> 00:01:58.639
I don't know how he
pronounces his name.

00:02:00.798 --> 00:02:03.878
And a... Oh!
No, before I go into that one.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:08.228
There was a discussion
of Michel Thomas.

00:02:09.251 --> 00:02:14.545
Of his very successful method.
He has sold lots.

00:02:14.545 --> 00:02:19.758
He taught movie stars and charged
a fortune for doing so.

00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:23.474
I have listened to Michel
Thomas trying to teach...

00:02:23.474 --> 00:02:26.934
or teaching German, which
is his native language.

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:30.973
He also does them in Italian
and Spanish and French.

00:02:30.973 --> 00:02:35.955
Where apparently he has a pretty bad accent,
because his accent in English is a...

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:40.289
...sort of a very strong
Central European accent.

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:42.776
But he's been
tremendously successful.

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:47.665
And... But I listened to this
explanation to his theory and...

00:02:47.912 --> 00:02:49.936
Yeah, I mean, I'm not...

00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:52.700
It is obvious that different
people learn in different ways,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:58.212
and many people are very happy with
Michel Thomas' and his method of teaching.

00:02:59.047 --> 00:03:01.452
But there's many things that I just don't agree with.

00:03:01.452 --> 00:03:06.993
And he says, for example, that there are no
bad students, there are only bad teachers.

00:03:07.295 --> 00:03:10.053
If you can't learn it's my fault, the teacher.

00:03:10.307 --> 00:03:12.649
Well, I just don't believe that.

00:03:12.649 --> 00:03:15.085
I think the teacher is irrelevant.

00:03:15.498 --> 00:03:20.466
I wouldn't say irrelevant, but the teacher in
terms of teaching the language is not necessary.

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.268
The teacher as a...

00:03:22.839 --> 00:03:27.347
...as a resource, as a person who encourages,
as a person who provides feedback,

00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:31.561
who inspires, who stimulates;
all of those things, yes.

00:03:31.561 --> 00:03:34.490
And I see that more as
the role of a tutor,

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:38.267
as someone looking over someone else,
someone who is, already, learning languages

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:42.069
and is providing some support and
encouragement of someone else.

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:43.561
All of that is great.

00:03:43.561 --> 00:03:46.259
Teaching the language;
I just don't see it.

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.974
I think you'll learn the
language from the language.

00:03:49.561 --> 00:03:54.220
He works very hard on getting
people to say things very quickly.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.228
But he doesn't cover
a lot of vocabulary,

00:03:56.228 --> 00:03:59.331
and to me the whole thing
about language learning,

00:03:59.331 --> 00:04:01.862
and if you want to be
able to speak and...

00:04:01.862 --> 00:04:04.505
converse and be comfortable
in any situations,

00:04:04.505 --> 00:04:07.252
you need a lot of words.
He doesn't cover a lot of words.

00:04:07.252 --> 00:04:10.545
He tries to get you to speak
from the beginning, ok.

00:04:10.545 --> 00:04:13.639
Some people want to do that.
It's not what I want to do.

00:04:13.639 --> 00:04:20.267
I'm not interested in constructing whole
sentences early, just so that I can say...

00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:24.161
You know, what is the color
of your white horse.

00:04:24.961 --> 00:04:26.407
I want to learn words.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.700
Some of the things that I do agree
with, he says it's important to relax.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:37.287
I think there are many people who create these mental
blocks for themselves, that they are too nervous.

00:04:37.287 --> 00:04:42.316
To relax, to enjoy the language.
He says "Get out of you own way", I agree.

00:04:42.316 --> 00:04:45.655
Sometimes people are their own worst enemy
they are hung up

00:04:45.655 --> 00:04:50.663
about grammar, they're hung up about
how they sound, they're hung up about

00:04:50.663 --> 00:04:54.091
being able to get out of their
own culture, and so forth.

00:04:54.091 --> 00:04:55.751
So, I agree with all of that.

00:04:55.751 --> 00:04:59.615
Another major part of his
approach is that: no reading.

00:04:59.615 --> 00:05:03.115
Well, I think reading is a
tremendous way to access

00:05:03.115 --> 00:05:07.272
the culture on the language
and learn words and so forth.

00:05:07.856 --> 00:05:17.378
So, Michel Thomas, obviously, is very popular and...
But so there was a youtube of a lady explaining his system.

00:05:17.378 --> 00:05:24.203
Now, there was another series of
youtubes where a person by the name of

00:05:24.203 --> 00:05:33.069
Professor Alexander Arguelles reviews a number
of the more popular language learning books.

00:05:33.069 --> 00:05:39.936
Teach Yourself, Assimil, Colloquial,
I don't know if he did Pimsleur, Hugo.

00:05:39.936 --> 00:05:45.538
He goes literally through all of them.
And he gives you a sort of a nine minute explanation,

00:05:45.538 --> 00:05:51.213
going back to the earlier versions
of the 1920's and the 1930's.

00:05:53.348 --> 00:05:57.522
Interesting if you are interested in the
history of different language learning methods.

00:05:58.251 --> 00:06:05.774
He deplores the fact that the methods are becoming
simpler, with a greater emphasis on phrases.

00:06:05.774 --> 00:06:11.971
And I agree with him there.
You can't teach through unconnected phrases.

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:15.285
You want interesting dialogue.

00:06:15.905 --> 00:06:19.262
He likes the idea that some of the
earlier versions of some of these

00:06:19.262 --> 00:06:23.928
systems, seem to follow the same format in
every language, so that they would teach

00:06:23.928 --> 00:06:29.539
pronouns in lesson 1, verbs in lesson 2
and numbers in lesson 3 and so forth.

00:06:30.379 --> 00:06:34.105
To me that's of a limited benefit,
because every language is different,

00:06:34.105 --> 00:06:37.217
some language don't have articles,
some languages don't have the

00:06:37.217 --> 00:06:39.509
same range of tenses
as other languages.

00:06:39.851 --> 00:06:43.620
He suggested that by
learning language A,

00:06:43.907 --> 00:06:49.819
if the second book follows the same format that will
help to learn language B, I don't see that at all.

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:53.816
I don't think it really matters in
what order you learn the grammar.

00:06:55.745 --> 00:07:00.641
You learn through interesting content.
Some grammar concepts, in say,

00:07:00.641 --> 00:07:04.640
Japanese or Chinese or Russian, are
going to stick early, some later.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.148
With some people than withÉ..
I don't see that at all.

00:07:07.318 --> 00:07:11.147
He is very found of the, sort of,
artificial scenarios:

00:07:11.207 --> 00:07:14.947
at the bank, at the post office,
at the train station,

00:07:14.957 --> 00:07:19.013
and I've always felt those to be...
to the extent that those dialogues

00:07:19.013 --> 00:07:23.556
are artificial they're not that useful, I mean,
you need obviously in the beginning,

00:07:23.556 --> 00:07:28.450
you need some artificial dialogue,
as you can't deal with authentic content.

00:07:28.785 --> 00:07:31.226
It almost doesn't matter
what that artificial dialogue is,

00:07:31.236 --> 00:07:32.198
they can be
on anything.

00:07:32.208 --> 00:07:35.484
It doesn't have to be at the train station.
You won't, or the the bank,

00:07:35.484 --> 00:07:38.618
you won't be able to operate at bank
based on the scenario you learn.

00:07:38.881 --> 00:07:42.218
So, you could be learning anything.
It could be, you know, "How are you?",

00:07:42.234 --> 00:07:44.545
"What did you do with your children today?",
it could be anything.

00:07:44.555 --> 00:07:48.529
It really doesn't matter.
So having, sort of, parallel scenarios

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:51.039
that he talks about,
I don't see the benefit.

00:07:51.254 --> 00:07:56.416
He talks about some of them having a
30 page glossary at the end of the book

00:07:56.425 --> 00:07:59.776
and therefore you've learned all these words.
Well no, you haven't learn the words

00:07:59.766 --> 00:08:02.087
because there's a glossary,
that's not how you learn words.

00:08:02.107 --> 00:08:04.375
You can't learn words
by reading a glossary.

00:08:04.468 --> 00:08:07.782
The fact that some of the dialogues
have all these words in them, are fine.

00:08:08.228 --> 00:08:12.639
There's just so much better ways
now to deal with learning words;

00:08:12.639 --> 00:08:15.458
some of the things we think we're
doing at LingQ that are good.

00:08:15.536 --> 00:08:19.482
I have never been able to
learn words from a glossary.

00:08:19.574 --> 00:08:22.938
It's distracting to keep
on having to look back

00:08:23.688 --> 00:08:28.305
to a glossary; that's the
advantage of reading on the web.

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:32.989
But I'm going to,
on my blog post,

00:08:32.989 --> 00:08:39.987
I'm going to post the URL for his
website and for his youtubes.

00:08:39.976 --> 00:08:43.289
And one of the things he's very fond of,
is what he calls shadowing,

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:48.882
where you walk around, you know,
reciting these phrases and so forth and...

00:08:48.882 --> 00:08:55.220
I mean, I'm sure it works for people,
doesn't work for me, but everyone is different.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:59.319
So yeah, I just put these...
mention these things

00:08:59.329 --> 00:09:07.333
so that you can have a look and see what you...
what you think. I think it... a lot of this is...

00:09:07.333 --> 00:09:11.568
It just points out again to me how different
people have different ways of learning,

00:09:11.568 --> 00:09:15.379
but I think it might be of interest
to some people to explore.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:21.484
Some of the YouTubes that seem to collect
around my Youtubes for, you know,

00:09:21.484 --> 00:09:27.119
whatever reason having to do with
how Youtube organizes itself.

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:30.896
So, if you go to the blog post
you will see some of these URLs.

00:09:30.906 --> 00:09:32.880
Thank you for listening.

